1
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:18,361
JAMIE: Dow's up three point zero three percent this morning, but Nvidia just dropped three point one seven—turns out the post-election rally wants to celebrate everything except the company that actually builds the chips everyone's been obsessed with.

2
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:31,008
ALEX: The entire chip sector's repricing on election uncertainty. Broadcom down two percent, and the irony is that exposure to cloud infrastructure and AI spending is exactly what made some chips the hero yesterday, and it's exactly what makes them vulnerable today.

3
00:00:31,008 --> 00:01:00,516
ALEX: So the breadth of today's rally is genuinely strange. S&P up one point five eight percent to 7688.62, Dow up three point zero three to 53356.12, but the Nasdaq only up one point four four. Microsoft, McDonald's, Home Depot all up six point five percent or better. Amazon up six point one two. Those are old-economy wins—things you can touch, eat, or renovate. Meanwhile, oil stocks are getting hammered—Exxon down four point six two, Chevron down three point six nine—and the energy complex is basically pricing in recession or a trade war that kills demand. Or both.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/04/mcdonalds-is-about-to-report-earnings-heres-what-to-expect.html

4
00:01:00,516 --> 00:01:11,054
JAMIE: So it's like the market is hedging itself by celebrating the companies that won't care if the economy slows, but absolutely terrified of what happens to the commodity chain if tariffs actually kick in.

5
00:01:11,054 --> 00:01:20,087
MAYA: And Tesla's down three point three four percent. Bitcoin up though, so at least the people who thought the election would cause chaos have a hedge that's actually working.

6
00:01:20,087 --> 00:01:38,756
ALEX: The boom is real. The projects already funded are real. But chip makers priced in a tailwind where they're the only suppliers that can be trusted, and they get to price accordingly. If tariffs don't materialize, if restrictions soften, if the capex keeps flowing but at lower margins because you're competing on price again instead of geopolitics—that's a very different business.

7
00:01:38,756 --> 00:01:50,800
JAMIE: So it's not that chip demand dies—it's that the chip makers priced in a protection racket that might not happen. And now they're trading on fundamentals instead of policy fantasy, which is apparently a three to six percent haircut.

8
00:01:50,800 --> 00:02:01,640
MAYA: Figma beat earnings, stock dropped anyway—the company's shifting the entire product toward AI features, same subscription price, and Wall Street's response was basically 'we wanted you to charge more for that, not give it away.'

9
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:07,963
JAMIE: So they're saying 'here's more value' and the market heard 'here's less margin' and just walked out of the room.

10
00:02:07,963 --> 00:02:24,523
MAYA: Exactly. Investors want to see the AI tax immediately. They want to see a new tier, a new button, a new price point. Giving it to existing customers is like showing up to a negotiation with a better offer and watching the other side just leave because you didn't ask for more money first.

11
00:02:24,523 --> 00:02:34,761
ALEX: McDonald's crushed earnings—same-store sales up, franchisees are still complaining about labor costs eating into margins, but the stock's up six point five three percent because the U.S. consumer is apparently still showing up.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/04/mcdonalds-is-about-to-report-earnings-heres-what-to-expect.html

12
00:02:34,761 --> 00:02:39,277
JAMIE: Wait, so the stock's celebrating that people are still poor enough to need discounts?

13
00:02:39,277 --> 00:02:54,333
ALEX: The stock's celebrating that they found a way to pack restaurants without raising prices, which means volumes are up and the company's making more money per location even though the franchisees are squeezing every cost center they can find. It's a beautiful separation of incentives—corporate wins, franchisees get tighter.

14
00:02:54,333 --> 00:03:01,860
MAYA: CVS is spinning off the drugstore business entirely, stock down ten percent, and the entire thesis of 'vertically integrated healthcare conglomerate' just got reversed.

15
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:11,194
JAMIE: So they paid billions to learn that Wall Street doesn't want complexity, it wants a stock that does one thing really well and doesn't require a flowchart to explain it.

16
00:03:11,194 --> 00:03:24,443
MAYA: AI PACs spent millions trying to shape Congress and mostly lost—like, they had venture capital, they had polling data, they had every tech CEO's personal vendetta wrapped up in a donation vehicle, and Congress still went the direction Congress wanted to go anyway.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/ai-pacs-spent-millions-trying-to-shape-congress-heres-how-they-fared/

17
00:03:24,443 --> 00:03:32,874
JAMIE: So they learned that money doesn't actually buy elections, it just buys the privilege of losing more publicly and having to explain it to your LP deck.

18
00:03:32,874 --> 00:03:41,003
ALEX: Nvidia's three point one seven percent down is what happens when you're priced as the country's industrial policy, and policy just got a lot more complicated.

19
00:03:41,003 --> 00:03:46,122
MAYA: So they went from 'inevitable' to 'depends on what Congress does next' in one trading day.

20
00:03:46,122 --> 00:03:50,338
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
